---
import BaseLayout from "@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---
<BaseLayout title="Zeugnis" headTitle="About Zeugnis">
  <figure class="banner">
    <img src="/assets/img/zeugnis/ram.png" alt="Random Access Memories by Daft Punk" />
  </figure>

  <div class="index-title">Welcome to Random Access Memories!</div>
  <p><em>The banner above features the album Random Access Memories by Daft Punk.</em></p>

  <h2>Why “Random Access Memories”?</h2>
  <p>
    The name of this blog, Random Access Memories, is a homage to both the iconic Daft Punk album and the electronic
    computer memory. I hope this blog works as a space to capture and revisit thoughts, languages, music, and random
    memories or sort. It’s less of a structured archive and more of a patchwork of experiences, reflections, and
    observations. Each post is from a small part of my memory, archived in a manner so that myself and any of you can
    access it freely.
  </p>

  <h2>Why “Zeugnis”?</h2>
  <p>
    One of my nicknames that I choose to use — Zeugnis, comes from the German word Zeugnis /ˈtsɔɪ̯k.nɪs/. In
    contemporary German, it usually means <em>certificate</em> or <em>report card</em>, otherwise <em>testimony</em> or
    <em>witness declaration</em>. Meanwhile, Zeugnis in my nickname actually comes from Johann Sebastian Bach’s church
    cantata <em>Herz und Mund und Tat und Leben, BWV 147</em>, famous for the tenth movement chorale
    <em>Jesus bleibet meine Freude</em>. The term Zeugnis appears in the opening chorus
    <em>Herz und Mund und Tat und Leben</em> as follows.
  </p>

  <div class="epigraph">
    <blockquote>
      <p>
        Muß von Christo Zeugniß geben <em>(original orthography)</em><br />
        Muß von Christo Zeugnis geben <em>(current orthgraphy)</em>
      </p>
    </blockquote>
  </div>

  <p>
    This literally means ‘must bear witness to Christ’. While I am not a religious person, I have interests in religious
    studies and also like making connections with religious motifs — and this is how I came up with my nickname.
  </p>

  <h2>What’s on the favicon?</h2>
  <p>
    The favicon changes from time to time, so I keep a small archive here<label
      for="favicons"
      class="margin-toggle">&#8853;</label
    ><input type="checkbox" id="favicons" class="margin-toggle" checked /><span class="marginnote">
      <img class="fullwidth" src="/favicons/zeugnis.png" alt="" /><br />Current favicon as of 2026-05-09.<br /><br />
      <img class="fullwidth" src="/assets/img/zeugnis/kun.png" alt="" /><br />Previous favicon, used from 2025-10-10
      to 2026-05-09.
    </span>.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>2026-05-09–present:</strong> a black circular mark (a mark based on my name in Chinese characters), now used as the blog favicon.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>2025-10-10–2026-05-09:</strong> a Chinese seal with the letter 灌 on it. Specifically made by a friend,
      this letter is related to my nickname. It was digitalized from an actual usage of the seal using Photoshop.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>About the owner</h2>
  <p>I am <em>Zeugnis</em> from Korea. My posts are likely to talk about:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>Languages: <em>I love linguistics and languages</em></li>
    <li>
      Typography and Calligrpahy:
      <em>I love all types of art related to letters — including typography and calligraphy</em>
    </li>
    <li>
      Food Culture:
      <em>Exploring diverse cuisines and understanding the stories behind them is one of my greatest passions.</em>
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>and maybe much more.</p>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  /* This page opens on the banner rather than an <h1>, so nothing supplies the
